--- !u!114 &1993674690
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1993674688}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 0
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Text Digitization:

    In large language models, computers cannot directly
    understand text. Therefore, natural language must first be converted into a data
    format that computers can process.


    After simple text cleaning, which ensures
    the data is standardized, text normalization is performed. Once all preparatory
    work is completed, the process moves on to tokenization.


    Depending on how
    the text is segmented, there are generally three types of tokens:

    word-level,
    character-level, and subword-level.

    In this work, we adopt a word-level
    tokenization approach.


    After obtaining tokens, the model cannot directly
    use them; they must be mapped to numbers, i.e., Token IDs. This process relies
    on an existing vocabulary and converts instructions into a sequence of integers.


    However,
    Token IDs cannot be directly understood by neural networks (we will explain neural
    networks in detail later). Since an ID is merely an index, it carries no mathematical
    meaning. To allow the computer to understand the relationships between words,
    large language models convert these discrete numbers into vectors that encode
    semantic information. This process is called vectorization or embedding.


    These
    vectors exist in a high-dimensional semantic space, where words with similar
    meanings usually have similar vector representations. Through embeddings, the
    semantic relationships in language are encoded as distances in a mathematical
    space. In this way, the semantic relationships between words are encoded into
    the vector space, enabling neural networks to capture the semantic structure
    of language through mathematical operations.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
